<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1226">1226</a>–<a href="/years/1260">1260</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd was atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salghurids">Salghurid dynasty</a> from 1226 to 1260. By submitting to the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei and paying tribute, he saved Fars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> from destruction. The poet Saadi, whose pen name honoured the Salghurids, dedicated the Bustan (1257) and the Gulistan (1258) to the dynasty during his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-sad.jpg" alt="Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd" width="301" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1604, Mughal artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Bakr_b._Sa%60d_b._Zangi_Folio_from_the_Manuscript_of_Kulliyat_(Collected_Works)_of_Sa%60di_(d._1292)_Accession_Number_AKM284.15.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Bakr, also known as Muzaffar al-Din Qutlugh Khan, was the son of Sa'd I (r. 1198–1226), atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, a region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> ruled by the Turkoman Salghurid dynasty since 1148. While his father was at war with two princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, Abu Bakr rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> in a bid for power, but he was defeated by local troops and imprisoned. He was released just before his father's death on 5 November 1226 and succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 12 November 1230 he captured Kish Island, which gave him access to the trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the dues he could levy on it. In August 1236 he seized the islands of Bahrayn, officially part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, which led to a long struggle with their Arab inhabitants. After the Ilkhanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a> occupied northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, Abu Bakr declared his allegiance by sending his son Sa'd II to the Mongol court at Baghdad.</p><p>He took a strong interest in learning and gathered scholars and artists at his court, among them the poet Saadi Shirazi. Culture flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> in these years, and he built many hospitals and religious institutions that drew scholars. According to local tradition his minister, Amir Muqarrab al-Din Mas'ud, found the burial place of Ahmad, a brother of the eighth Imam Ali al-Ridha, identified by his seal ring, and Abu Bakr had a nearby hill dug where the body of Ahmad's brother Husayn was said to lie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abu Bakr died on 18 May 1260 while Sa'd II was at the Mongol court. Sa'd II succeeded him but died of illness at Tafresh on the way, after ruling for twelve or eighteen days. The modern historian John Limbert dates the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salghurids">Salghurids</a> from Abu Bakr's death, describing his successors as drunkards, braggarts or children, unlike the harsh and tough-minded Salghurid rulers before them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd</b> submitted to the Mongol Great Khan Ögedei, paying tribute and keeping Fars from destruction.</blockquote><a href="/state/salghurid-dynasty">Salghurid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
